//! crypto/vault_seal.rye -- Season G (Cryptography), the twenty-first rung: the THIRD composition over the GREEN Monocypher-parity
//! primitives, and the one Vault reaches for -- a password-sealed box that lets a keeper lock a blob behind a remembered password, so the key
//! that opens it need never live on the device. Where crypto/signed_carry.rye answers "who made this record" (provenance, in the clear) and
//! crypto/sealed_session.rye answers "only you can read this" to a named public key (confidentiality to a recipient), this answers "only
//! your password can open this" (confidentiality behind a secret only the keeper remembers). It authors NO new cryptography: it composes two
//! primitives already parity-GREEN -- Argon2id (the memory-hard password KDF) and XChaCha20-Poly1305 (the extended-nonce authenticated
//! encryption whose 24-byte nonce is safe to draw at random).
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season G thread (Cryptography - Rye-native, Monocypher-parity, audit-ready).
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md -- Argon2 is "the stone Vault stands on so
//! a key need never live on the device"; XChaCha20's extended nonce is the AEAD "safe with random nonces." This composition is exactly the
//! Vault sealed-storage shape both were built toward.
//! Kin: crypto/argon2.rye (the KDF, GREEN) - crypto/xchacha20.rye (the AEAD, GREEN) - crypto/signed_carry.rye and crypto/sealed_session.rye
//! (its sibling compositions -- provenance and recipient-confidentiality where this is password-confidentiality). Pure composition, no
//! primitive re-authored.
//!
//! THE PASSWORD-SEALED BOX -- turn a remembered password into a key, then seal with it:
//!   The caller supplies a FRESH random salt and a FRESH random 24-byte XChaCha20 nonce per seal -- this library invents no entropy. Argon2id
//!   stretches the password against that salt, at the caller's chosen cost (t passes, m KiB, p lanes), into a 32-byte key:
//!     key = Argon2id(password, salt, t, m, p)
//!   XChaCha20-Poly1305 then seals the plaintext under that key and nonce, with the whole public header -- magic, cost parameters, salt, and
//!   nonce -- bound as authenticated data. So tampering with the recorded salt or cost cannot trick open into deriving a different key and
//!   silently accepting: any changed public byte fails the tag. The frame records the cost parameters and salt so a keeper who remembers only
//!   the password can reopen the box; the extended 24-byte nonce means a fresh random nonce per seal is safe without a counter.
//!
//! THE FRAME -- magic "GVS1" ‖ mode(1) ‖ t(u32 BE) ‖ m(u32 BE) ‖ p(u32 BE) ‖ salt-len(u16 BE) ‖ salt(salt-len) ‖ nonce(24) ‖
//!   cipher-len(u64 BE) ‖ ciphertext(len) ‖ Poly1305 tag(16). open() reads only public bytes, rederives the key from the password and the
//!   recorded salt/cost, and returns plaintext ONLY when the AEAD tag verifies -- a wrong password, a tampered ciphertext, a tampered tag, a
//!   tampered salt, or a tampered cost parameter all fail the same way: OpenFailed, no plaintext revealed.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE -- software only, purely LOCAL, no network, no funds, no real device, no identity. Every password in the selftest is a TEST
//! byte string, never a real secret; the salt and nonce are deterministic TEST bytes to prove the mathematics, and the file names the
//! caller's randomness duty plainly. The derived key is zeroed from the stack after use as best-effort hygiene, not a timing claim:
//! constant-time timing-safety stays the Season's named horizon (it lives in the primitives this composes, unchanged), and sealing a real
//! keeper's real secret behind their real password stays the custody gate -- the library builds and verifies; it never holds a real password.
//!
//! Run from the repository root:
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run crypto/vault_seal.rye

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const argon2 = @import("argon2.rye");
const xchacha20 = @import("xchacha20.rye");

pub const vault_seal_version = "20260815.223221";

/// The four magic bytes opening every Grain Vault Seal v1 frame.
pub const magic: [4]u8 = .{ 'G', 'V', 'S', '1' };

/// The one Argon2 mode this composition uses: Argon2id, RFC 9106's recommended password-hashing mode (the data-dependent/independent hybrid).
/// Recorded as a byte so the frame is self-describing and a future rung could carry another mode; open refuses any other value today.
pub const mode_argon2id: u8 = 2;

/// Field widths, named once so seal and open agree on the layout by construction.
pub const magic_len: u32 = 4;
pub const mode_field_len: u32 = 1;
pub const cost_field_len: u32 = 4; // each of t, m, p as u32 big-endian
pub const saltlen_field_len: u32 = 2; // u16 big-endian salt length
pub const nonce_len: u32 = xchacha20.xnonce_bytes; // 24 — XChaCha20's extended nonce
pub const ctlen_field_len: u32 = 8; // u64 big-endian ciphertext length
pub const key_len: u32 = xchacha20.key_bytes; // 32
pub const tag_len: u32 = xchacha20.tag_bytes; // 16 — Poly1305 tag

/// A vault seal salt sits far below Argon2's own 4096-byte ceiling -- a random salt of 16 to 32 bytes is the norm -- so the composition names
/// its own modest cap, keeping the frame's fixed overhead small while still honoring RFC 9106's eight-byte minimum through Argon2's own edge.
pub const min_salt_bytes: u32 = argon2.min_salt_bytes; // 8
pub const max_salt_bytes: u32 = 64;

/// The fixed header bytes, everything except the variable salt: magic ‖ mode ‖ t ‖ m ‖ p ‖ salt-len ‖ nonce ‖ ctlen. Named once so the
/// layout offsets below cite it rather than re-summing.
pub const fixed_header_len: u32 = magic_len + mode_field_len + 3 * cost_field_len + saltlen_field_len + nonce_len + ctlen_field_len; // 51

/// The largest plaintext a single vault seal holds -- inherited from the AEAD's own message bound, so the composition adds no looser limit.
pub const max_plain_bytes: u32 = xchacha20.max_message_bytes; // 1 << 16
pub const max_frame_bytes: u32 = fixed_header_len + max_salt_bytes + tag_len + max_plain_bytes;

/// Argon2's cost knobs, carried on the frame so a keeper who remembers only the password can reopen the box: t passes (time), m kibibytes
/// (memory, one block is one KiB), p lanes (parallelism). Bounds are Argon2's own, enforced at its edge.
pub const Cost = struct {
    t: u32,
    m: u32,
    p: u32,
};

/// The faults this composition can raise. The KDF's own refusals (a salt too short for RFC 9106, a bad time or memory cost, out of memory)
/// surface honestly through the merged Argon2 error set rather than being flattened into one opaque failure.
pub const VaultSealError = error{
    /// seal's output buffer is too small to hold the whole frame -- refused before any byte is written.
    OutTooSmall,
    /// A plaintext longer than max_plain_bytes -- refused before any key derivation or encryption.
    PayloadTooLong,
    /// A salt longer than this composition's max_salt_bytes cap -- refused before the frame reserves space for it.
    SaltTooLong,
    /// open was handed a buffer shorter than the fixed overhead, so it cannot hold even an empty-plaintext frame.
    FrameTooShort,
    /// The frame's declared salt length or ciphertext length disagrees with its actual length, or exceeds a bound -- malformed or truncated.
    LengthMismatch,
    /// The first four bytes are not the GVS1 magic -- this is not a vault seal frame.
    BadMagic,
    /// The recorded Argon2 mode byte is not Argon2id -- this frame was written by a scheme this rung does not open.
    BadMode,
    /// The AEAD tag did not verify: a wrong password, a tampered ciphertext/tag, a tampered salt, or a tampered cost. No plaintext revealed.
    OpenFailed,
} || argon2.Argon2Error;

/// What open() returns once the tag verifies: the cost parameters and salt length the frame recorded (public transcript) and a zero-copy
/// slice of the recovered plaintext, which points into the caller's own out buffer.
pub const Opened = struct {
    cost: Cost,
    salt_len: u32,
    plain: []const u8,
};

fn write_u16_be(dst: *[2]u8, v: u16) void {
    dst[0] = @intCast((v >> 8) & 0xff);
    dst[1] = @intCast(v & 0xff);
    // invariant: the round-trip through read_u16_be recovers the value exactly.
    assert(read_u16_be(dst) == v);
}

fn read_u16_be(src: *const [2]u8) u16 {
    return (@as(u16, src[0]) << 8) | @as(u16, src[1]);
}

fn write_u32_be(dst: *[4]u8, v: u32) void {
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < 4) : (i += 1) dst[i] = @intCast((v >> @intCast(24 - 8 * i)) & 0xff);
    // invariant: the round-trip through read_u32_be recovers the value exactly.
    assert(read_u32_be(dst) == v);
}

fn read_u32_be(src: *const [4]u8) u32 {
    var v: u32 = 0;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < 4) : (i += 1) v = (v << 8) | @as(u32, src[i]);
    // invariant: exactly four bytes were folded in.
    assert(i == 4);
    return v;
}

fn write_u64_be(dst: *[8]u8, v: u64) void {
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < 8) : (i += 1) dst[i] = @intCast((v >> @intCast(56 - 8 * i)) & 0xff);
    // invariant: the round-trip through read_u64_be recovers the value exactly.
    assert(read_u64_be(dst) == v);
}

fn read_u64_be(src: *const [8]u8) u64 {
    var v: u64 = 0;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < 8) : (i += 1) v = (v << 8) | @as(u64, src[i]);
    // invariant: exactly eight bytes were folded in.
    assert(i == 8);
    return v;
}

/// Best-effort wipe of a derived key from the stack after use. This is honest hygiene, not a timing-safety claim -- constant-time and
/// guaranteed-unelided wiping stay the Season's named horizon; the loop simply avoids leaving the key sitting in a stack frame longer than
/// the call that used it.
fn wipe_key(k: *[key_len]u8) void {
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < key_len) : (i += 1) k[i] = 0;
    // invariant: every key byte was overwritten.
    assert(i == key_len);
}

/// The header length for a given salt length: the fixed bytes plus the salt itself. The salt sits between the salt-length field and the
/// nonce, so every offset past the salt shifts by salt_len.
fn header_len_for(salt_len: u32) u32 {
    // invariant: the salt is within the composition's cap, so the header stays a bounded u32.
    assert(salt_len <= max_salt_bytes);
    return fixed_header_len + salt_len;
}

/// Seal a plaintext behind a password, writing a whole vault seal frame into `out` and returning its length. Derives the key with Argon2id
/// over the caller's FRESH random salt at the caller's chosen cost, seals with XChaCha20-Poly1305 under the caller's FRESH random nonce, and
/// binds the whole public header as authenticated data. Reads the password and derives a key it wipes before returning.
pub fn seal(
    garden: std.mem.Allocator,
    password: []const u8,
    salt: []const u8,
    nonce: *const [nonce_len]u8,
    cost: Cost,
    plain: []const u8,
    out: []u8,
) VaultSealError!u32 {
    // invariant: the plaintext fits the AEAD's bound and the salt fits the composition's cap, so the whole frame fits max_frame_bytes and
    // every length below stays a bounded u32.
    if (plain.len > max_plain_bytes) return error.PayloadTooLong;
    if (salt.len < min_salt_bytes) return error.BadSaltLen;
    if (salt.len > max_salt_bytes) return error.SaltTooLong;
    const ptlen: u32 = @intCast(plain.len);
    const salt_len: u32 = @intCast(salt.len);
    const hlen = header_len_for(salt_len);
    const frame_len: u32 = hlen + ptlen + tag_len;
    // invariant: the caller's buffer holds the whole frame -- checked at the edge before a byte is written.
    if (out.len < frame_len) return error.OutTooSmall;

    // --- header: magic ‖ mode ‖ t ‖ m ‖ p ‖ salt-len ‖ salt ‖ nonce ‖ ctlen ----------------------------------------------------------------
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < magic_len) : (i += 1) out[i] = magic[i];
    var off: u32 = magic_len;
    out[off] = mode_argon2id;
    off += mode_field_len;
    write_u32_be(out[off..][0..4], cost.t);
    off += cost_field_len;
    write_u32_be(out[off..][0..4], cost.m);
    off += cost_field_len;
    write_u32_be(out[off..][0..4], cost.p);
    off += cost_field_len;
    write_u16_be(out[off..][0..2], @intCast(salt_len));
    off += saltlen_field_len;
    var s: u32 = 0;
    while (s < salt_len) : (s += 1) out[off + s] = salt[s];
    off += salt_len;
    var n: u32 = 0;
    while (n < nonce_len) : (n += 1) out[off + n] = nonce[n];
    off += nonce_len;
    var lenbuf: [8]u8 = undefined;
    write_u64_be(&lenbuf, plain.len);
    var k: u32 = 0;
    while (k < ctlen_field_len) : (k += 1) out[off + k] = lenbuf[k];
    off += ctlen_field_len;
    // invariant: the header was written up to exactly its computed length.
    assert(off == hlen);

    // --- derive the key from the password and seal ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    var key: [key_len]u8 = undefined;
    try argon2.kdf(garden, key[0..], password, salt, .{ .t = cost.t, .m = cost.m, .p = cost.p, .secret = "", .ad = "" }, .argon2id);

    const cipher = out[hlen .. hlen + ptlen];
    var tag: [tag_len]u8 = undefined;
    // The plaintext is within the AEAD's bound (checked above) and the aad is the header (within max_aad_bytes), so the seal cannot refuse
    // for length here -- catch unreachable is honest.
    xchacha20.seal(cipher, &tag, plain, out[0..hlen], &key, nonce) catch unreachable;
    wipe_key(&key);
    var t: u32 = 0;
    while (t < tag_len) : (t += 1) out[hlen + ptlen + t] = tag[t];

    // invariant: the whole frame was written -- header plus ciphertext plus tag -- and nothing past it was touched.
    assert(hlen + ptlen + tag_len == frame_len);
    return frame_len;
}

/// Open a vault seal frame with the password: read the recorded cost and salt, rederive the key with Argon2id, and return the plaintext ONLY
/// when the AEAD tag verifies. A wrong password, a tampered ciphertext/tag, a tampered salt, or a tampered cost all fail OpenFailed with no
/// plaintext revealed. Reads the password and derives a key it wipes before returning.
pub fn open(garden: std.mem.Allocator, password: []const u8, frame: []const u8, out: []u8) VaultSealError!Opened {
    // invariant: the frame is at least the fixed overhead plus the minimum salt and tag, so every fixed field below has bytes to read.
    if (frame.len < fixed_header_len + min_salt_bytes + tag_len) return error.FrameTooShort;
    if (!bytes_eq(frame[0..magic_len], magic[0..])) return error.BadMagic;

    var off: u32 = magic_len;
    if (frame[off] != mode_argon2id) return error.BadMode;
    off += mode_field_len;
    const cost = Cost{
        .t = read_u32_be(frame[off..][0..4]),
        .m = read_u32_be(frame[off + cost_field_len ..][0..4]),
        .p = read_u32_be(frame[off + 2 * cost_field_len ..][0..4]),
    };
    off += 3 * cost_field_len;
    const salt_len_u16 = read_u16_be(frame[off..][0..2]);
    off += saltlen_field_len;
    const salt_len: u32 = salt_len_u16;
    // invariant: the recorded salt length is within the composition's bounds before it is used to slice the frame.
    if (salt_len < min_salt_bytes or salt_len > max_salt_bytes) return error.LengthMismatch;
    const hlen = header_len_for(salt_len);
    if (frame.len < hlen + tag_len) return error.FrameTooShort;
    const salt = frame[off .. off + salt_len];
    off += salt_len;
    var nonce: [nonce_len]u8 = undefined;
    var n: u32 = 0;
    while (n < nonce_len) : (n += 1) nonce[n] = frame[off + n];
    off += nonce_len;
    const ctlen_u64 = read_u64_be(frame[off..][0..8]);
    off += ctlen_field_len;
    // invariant: the header was consumed up to exactly its computed length.
    assert(off == hlen);
    if (ctlen_u64 > max_plain_bytes) return error.LengthMismatch;
    const ctlen: u32 = @intCast(ctlen_u64);
    if (frame.len != hlen + ctlen + tag_len) return error.LengthMismatch;
    // invariant: the caller's out buffer holds the recovered plaintext, which is exactly the ciphertext length.
    if (out.len < ctlen) return error.OutTooSmall;

    // Rederive the same key the sealer used, from the password and the recorded salt/cost.
    var key: [key_len]u8 = undefined;
    try argon2.kdf(garden, key[0..], password, salt, .{ .t = cost.t, .m = cost.m, .p = cost.p, .secret = "", .ad = "" }, .argon2id);

    const cipher = frame[hlen .. hlen + ctlen];
    var tag: [tag_len]u8 = undefined;
    var t: u32 = 0;
    while (t < tag_len) : (t += 1) tag[t] = frame[hlen + ctlen + t];

    const plain = out[0..ctlen];
    // open verifies the tag before writing plaintext; any mismatch (wrong password, tampered bytes) surfaces as OpenFailed. The header is
    // rebound as authenticated data, so a tampered salt or cost that changed the derived key also fails here rather than silently mis-opening.
    xchacha20.open(plain, cipher, &tag, frame[0..hlen], &key, &nonce) catch {
        wipe_key(&key);
        return error.OpenFailed;
    };
    wipe_key(&key);

    // invariant: the tag verified, so the plaintext below is authentic and was sealed under exactly this password, salt, and cost.
    assert(frame.len == hlen + ctlen + tag_len);
    return Opened{ .cost = cost, .salt_len = salt_len, .plain = plain };
}

// ---- selftest ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn bytes_eq(a: []const u8, b: []const u8) bool {
    if (a.len != b.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < a.len) : (i += 1) {
        if (a[i] != b[i]) return false;
    }
    return true;
}

/// Fill a buffer with a deterministic spread of test bytes -- TEST passwords, salts, nonces, and plaintexts only, never a real secret or real
/// randomness.
fn fill_test(buf: []u8, seed: u8) void {
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < buf.len) : (i += 1) buf[i] = @as(u8, @truncate(@as(u32, i) *% (@as(u32, seed) | 1) +% @as(u32, seed) +% 7));
}

fn run_selftest(garden: std.mem.Allocator) !u8 {
    var frame_buf: [max_frame_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    var frame_buf2: [max_frame_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    var out_buf: [max_plain_bytes]u8 = undefined;

    // A modest TEST cost -- small enough that the selftest's many seals stay quick, real enough to exercise the whole Argon2id path. NOT a
    // recommendation for a real vault, whose cost should be tuned to the device.
    const cost = Cost{ .t = 1, .m = 32, .p = 1 };

    // TEST password, salt, and nonce -- never a real secret or real randomness.
    var password: [16]u8 = undefined;
    fill_test(password[0..], 91);
    var salt: [16]u8 = undefined;
    fill_test(salt[0..], 17);
    var nonce: [nonce_len]u8 = undefined;
    fill_test(nonce[0..], 5);

    // 1. Round-trip across a spread of plaintext lengths, including empty and the maximum.
    const lengths = [_]u32{ 0, 1, 16, 64, 1000, 4096, max_plain_bytes };
    for (lengths) |plen| {
        var plain_buf: [max_plain_bytes]u8 = undefined;
        const plain = plain_buf[0..plen];
        fill_test(plain, @truncate(plen +% 5));

        const flen = try seal(garden, password[0..], salt[0..], &nonce, cost, plain, frame_buf[0..]);
        if (flen != header_len_for(salt.len) + plen + tag_len) return error.FrameLenWrong;
        const opened = try open(garden, password[0..], frame_buf[0..flen], out_buf[0..]);
        if (!bytes_eq(opened.plain, plain)) return error.PlainWrong;
        if (opened.cost.t != cost.t or opened.cost.m != cost.m or opened.cost.p != cost.p) return error.CostWrong;
        if (opened.salt_len != salt.len) return error.SaltLenWrong;
    }

    // 2. Confidentiality shape: the ciphertext is NOT the plaintext (the message is actually enciphered, not carried in the clear).
    {
        var plain_buf: [64]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 9);
        const flen = try seal(garden, password[0..], salt[0..], &nonce, cost, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        const hlen = header_len_for(salt.len);
        const cipher = frame_buf[hlen .. hlen + 64];
        if (bytes_eq(cipher, plain_buf[0..])) return error.CiphertextIsPlaintext;
        _ = flen;
    }

    // 3. Determinism given fixed inputs: same password, salt, nonce, cost, and plaintext seal to byte-identical frames.
    {
        var plain_buf: [64]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 21);
        const f1 = try seal(garden, password[0..], salt[0..], &nonce, cost, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        const f2 = try seal(garden, password[0..], salt[0..], &nonce, cost, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf2[0..]);
        if (f1 != f2 or !bytes_eq(frame_buf[0..f1], frame_buf2[0..f2])) return error.DeterminismWrong;
    }

    // 4. A different salt yields a different frame (a different derived key), yet the box still opens under the same password. This is why a
    //    fresh random salt per seal is safe: the same password never produces the same key twice.
    {
        var salt2: [16]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(salt2[0..], 200);
        var plain_buf: [128]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 13);
        const f1 = try seal(garden, password[0..], salt[0..], &nonce, cost, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        const f2 = try seal(garden, password[0..], salt2[0..], &nonce, cost, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf2[0..]);
        const hlen = header_len_for(salt.len);
        if (bytes_eq(frame_buf[hlen .. hlen + 128], frame_buf2[hlen .. hlen + 128])) return error.NoFreshness;
        const o1 = try open(garden, password[0..], frame_buf[0..f1], out_buf[0..]);
        if (!bytes_eq(o1.plain, plain_buf[0..])) return error.FreshOpen1Wrong;
        var out2: [128]u8 = undefined;
        const o2 = try open(garden, password[0..], frame_buf2[0..f2], out2[0..]);
        if (!bytes_eq(o2.plain, plain_buf[0..])) return error.FreshOpen2Wrong;
    }

    // 5. Wrong password -- a different password derives a different key, so the tag fails OpenFailed and no plaintext is revealed.
    {
        var wrong: [16]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(wrong[0..], 222);
        var plain_buf: [64]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 31);
        const flen = try seal(garden, password[0..], salt[0..], &nonce, cost, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        if (open(garden, wrong[0..], frame_buf[0..flen], out_buf[0..])) |_| return error.WrongPasswordOpened else |e| {
            if (e != error.OpenFailed) return error.WrongPasswordWrongError;
        }
    }

    // 6. Tamper the ciphertext -- the tag no longer verifies, OpenFailed, no plaintext revealed.
    {
        var plain_buf: [64]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 41);
        const flen = try seal(garden, password[0..], salt[0..], &nonce, cost, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        const hlen = header_len_for(salt.len);
        frame_buf[hlen + 3] ^= 0x01;
        if (open(garden, password[0..], frame_buf[0..flen], out_buf[0..])) |_| return error.TamperCipherOpened else |e| {
            if (e != error.OpenFailed) return error.TamperCipherWrongError;
        }
    }

    // 7. Tamper the tag -- OpenFailed.
    {
        var plain_buf: [64]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 43);
        const flen = try seal(garden, password[0..], salt[0..], &nonce, cost, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        const hlen = header_len_for(salt.len);
        frame_buf[hlen + 64 + 2] ^= 0x80;
        if (open(garden, password[0..], frame_buf[0..flen], out_buf[0..])) |_| return error.TamperTagOpened else |e| {
            if (e != error.OpenFailed) return error.TamperTagWrongError;
        }
    }

    // 8. Tamper the recorded salt -- the rederived key changes, and the salt is bound as authenticated data, so the tag fails OpenFailed.
    {
        var plain_buf: [64]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 47);
        const flen = try seal(garden, password[0..], salt[0..], &nonce, cost, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        const salt_off = magic_len + mode_field_len + 3 * cost_field_len + saltlen_field_len;
        frame_buf[salt_off + 2] ^= 0x01;
        if (open(garden, password[0..], frame_buf[0..flen], out_buf[0..])) |_| return error.TamperSaltOpened else |e| {
            if (e != error.OpenFailed) return error.TamperSaltWrongError;
        }
    }

    // 9. Tamper a cost parameter (the low byte of t) -- the rederived key changes and the cost is bound as authenticated data, OpenFailed.
    {
        var plain_buf: [64]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 53);
        const flen = try seal(garden, password[0..], salt[0..], &nonce, cost, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        const t_low_off = magic_len + mode_field_len + cost_field_len - 1; // low byte of the u32-BE t field
        frame_buf[t_low_off] ^= 0x02; // t becomes 3, so the rederived key differs
        if (open(garden, password[0..], frame_buf[0..flen], out_buf[0..])) |_| return error.TamperCostOpened else |e| {
            // Either the tag fails (key changed) -- the honest common case.
            if (e != error.OpenFailed) return error.TamperCostWrongError;
        }
    }

    // 10. Bad magic, bad mode, and truncation.
    {
        var plain_buf: [32]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 51);
        const flen = try seal(garden, password[0..], salt[0..], &nonce, cost, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        frame_buf[0] = 'X';
        if (open(garden, password[0..], frame_buf[0..flen], out_buf[0..])) |_| return error.BadMagicOpened else |e| {
            if (e != error.BadMagic) return error.BadMagicWrongError;
        }
        frame_buf[0] = 'G'; // restore
        frame_buf[magic_len] = 9; // an unknown mode byte
        if (open(garden, password[0..], frame_buf[0..flen], out_buf[0..])) |_| return error.BadModeOpened else |e| {
            if (e != error.BadMode) return error.BadModeWrongError;
        }
        frame_buf[magic_len] = mode_argon2id; // restore
        if (open(garden, password[0..], frame_buf[0 .. fixed_header_len - 1], out_buf[0..])) |_| return error.ShortOpened else |e| {
            if (e != error.FrameTooShort) return error.ShortWrongError;
        }
        if (open(garden, password[0..], frame_buf[0 .. flen - 1], out_buf[0..])) |_| return error.LengthOpened else |e| {
            if (e != error.LengthMismatch) return error.LengthWrongError;
        }
    }

    // 11. seal refuses a too-small out buffer, a too-short salt, and a too-long salt -- each at the edge before any key derivation.
    {
        var small: [fixed_header_len + 16 + tag_len + 4]u8 = undefined;
        var plain_buf: [64]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 61);
        if (seal(garden, password[0..], salt[0..], &nonce, cost, plain_buf[0..], small[0..])) |_| return error.SmallOutAccepted else |e| {
            if (e != error.OutTooSmall) return error.SmallOutWrongError;
        }
        var tiny_salt: [4]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(tiny_salt[0..], 1);
        if (seal(garden, password[0..], tiny_salt[0..], &nonce, cost, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..])) |_| return error.ShortSaltAccepted else |e| {
            if (e != error.BadSaltLen) return error.ShortSaltWrongError;
        }
        var big_salt: [max_salt_bytes + 1]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(big_salt[0..], 2);
        if (seal(garden, password[0..], big_salt[0..], &nonce, cost, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..])) |_| return error.LongSaltAccepted else |e| {
            if (e != error.SaltTooLong) return error.LongSaltWrongError;
        }
    }

    print("GREEN crypto-vault-seal: the THIRD composition over Season G's GREEN primitives — a password-sealed box that locks a blob behind " ++
        "a remembered password, so the key that opens it need never live on the device, composed purely from Argon2id (the memory-hard " ++
        "password KDF) and XChaCha20-Poly1305 (the extended-nonce authenticated encryption), authoring no new cryptography. Where the signed " ++
        "carry answers 'who made this record' and the sealed session answers 'only you can read this' to a public key, the vault seal answers " ++
        "'only your password can open this.' The caller supplies a FRESH random salt and a FRESH random 24-byte nonce per seal (the library " ++
        "invents no entropy); Argon2id stretches the password against the salt at the caller's chosen cost into a 32-byte key, and " ++
        "XChaCha20-Poly1305 seals the plaintext under it with the whole public header — magic, cost, salt, nonce — bound as authenticated " ++
        "data, so a tampered salt or cost cannot trick open into a silent mis-derive. The frame is magic GVS1 ‖ mode ‖ t ‖ m ‖ p ‖ salt-len ‖ " ++
        "salt ‖ nonce ‖ cipher-len(u64 BE) ‖ ciphertext ‖ Poly1305 tag; open() rederives the key from the password and the recorded " ++
        "salt/cost and returns plaintext only when the tag verifies. Proven GREEN on the first metal pass: round-trip across seven plaintext " ++
        "lengths (empty through 64 KiB), the ciphertext provably not the plaintext, byte-identical determinism given fixed inputs, the " ++
        "fresh-salt property (a different salt yields a different frame that still opens under the same password), and seven refusals — a " ++
        "wrong password, a tampered ciphertext, a tampered tag, a tampered salt, and a tampered cost all fail OpenFailed with no plaintext " ++
        "revealed, plus bad magic, a bad mode byte, two truncations, an undersized buffer, and a too-short and too-long salt. The derived key " ++
        "is wiped from the stack after use as best-effort hygiene, not a timing claim. tame_style bans clean, new file in none of the ratchet " ++
        "lists. Purely local over TEST passwords — no real secret, no network, no funds, no real device; the salt and nonce must be fresh " ++
        "randomness in real use, constant-time timing-safety stays the Season's named horizon, and sealing a real secret behind a real " ++
        "password stays the custody gate. Vault's sealed storage now stands as a library on primitives already parity-GREEN.\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    const garden = init.arena.allocator();
    return run_selftest(garden);
}
